the inclusion of minority women in ice  hockey has been growing and for two  current stars and a legend of the game  their hockey journey started with family  and a supportive community i got  involved with hockey mainly for my dad  and his brother they played for team  canada ball hockey they decided to put  their kids in it they started with my  two brothers putting them in and put me  in dance but then decided later that i  was able to play hockey too because  girls can do that as well when i was  younger i was fortunate enough to have  an outdoor arena in my neighborhood  that's all we did as young kids was go  down to the outdoor arena and play my  dad grew up playing hockey in  scarborough ontario my dad's black my  mom's white and my dad really just had a  love for the game growing up and he  played ball hockey roller hockey  whatever it may be but he experienced  quite a bit of racism in the game and  that made him want to put his four kids  in it to prove people wrong so that's  how i got into playing hockey overcoming  racism and gender inequality angela  james emerged as the first superstar of  women's hockey and in 2010 she was  inducted into the hockey hall of fame  now an assistant coach with the toronto  six in the premier hockey federation  she's inspiring the next generation of  hockey superstars  growing up i always knew who angela  james was but i never realized how much  i needed her as a role model and to  actually be a physical figure in my life  since she was announced as the assistant  coach for the toronto six i remember  i literally cried when i found out she  was gonna be my assistant coach they  don't call her the wayne grexie of  women's hockey for no reason  being compared to wayne gretzky or being  called the wayne gretzky of women's  hockey is you know to me a very nice  honor it was given to me when i was in  college and it's  kind of stuck around now the next  generation of women hockey stars along  with their hall of fame coach are  looking to push the game forward and  make the ice more accessible and  inclusive for all i think having more  minorities is very important there's a  lot of in many age groups  starting from the professional league  all the way down to when kids are four  years old it's a sport for everyone no  matter your skin type whatever gender  you are minorities are not if they're  good  it would do great for the sport you know  the more people in the sport the better  anybody who's interested in hockey no  matter what race  no matter what sexual orientation should  be invited and it shouldn't really  matter of the color of your skin i want  to see more colorful faces in the arena  i love seeing my team walk into the  arena we are so diverse already here  with the toronto six and i mean it  brings me joy to get to interact with  other people in different cultures and  races and i mean i learn from them and i  think that we can all learn from each  other i think hockey is amazing and it  should be a community for all
 

